## Proof

Define, for any axis-parallel rectangle $S=[a,b]\times[c,d]$, the complex number

$$
I(S)=\iint_S e^{2\pi i(x+y)}\,dx\,dy
=\left(\int_a^b e^{2\pi i x}\,dx\right)\!\left(\int_c^d e^{2\pi i y}\,dy\right).
$$

**Key claim.** $I(S)=0$ if and only if $S$ has at least one side of integral length.

*Proof of claim.* Compute each factor:
$$
\int_a^b e^{2\pi i x}\,dx=\frac{e^{2\pi i b}-e^{2\pi i a}}{2\pi i}
=\frac{e^{2\pi i a}\bigl(e^{2\pi i(b-a)}-1\bigr)}{2\pi i}.
$$
This vanishes exactly when $e^{2\pi i(b-a)}=1$, i.e. when $b-a\in\mathbb{Z}$. Likewise the second factor vanishes exactly when $d-c\in\mathbb{Z}$. Since $I(S)$ is the product of the two factors, $I(S)=0$ iff $b-a\in\mathbb{Z}$ or $d-c\in\mathbb{Z}$, i.e. iff at least one side of $S$ has integral length. $\square$

Now consider the large rectangle $R$ and the decomposition $R=\bigcup_{i=1}^n R_i$ with pairwise disjoint interiors. The boundaries of the $R_i$ are finite unions of line segments, hence have (two-dimensional) measure zero, so

$$
\iint_R e^{2\pi i(x+y)}\,dx\,dy=\sum_{i=1}^n \iint_{R_i} e^{2\pi i(x+y)}\,dx\,dy,
$$

i.e. $I(R)=\sum_{i=1}^n I(R_i)$.

By hypothesis (iii), every $R_i$ has at least one side of integral length, so by the claim $I(R_i)=0$ for each $i$. Therefore

$$
I(R)=\sum_{i=1}^n 0 = 0.
$$

Applying the claim once more to $R$ itself, $I(R)=0$ forces $R$ to have at least one side of integral length. $\blacksquare$

